Linux find Command 

When servers get messy, the find command saves hours of manual searching.

🔍 File Search Examples

find . -name "*.log" → find all log files in the current directory

find /var/log -name "*.log" -type f → search log files under /var/log

find /home -user john -type f → list files owned by a specific user

find . -size +100M → locate large files (>100 MB)

find /tmp -mtime -1 → catch files modified in the last 24 hours

find . -name "config*" -exec ls -l {} \; → list details of config files

find /opt/myapp -name "*.conf" -o -name "*.yaml" → search config & YAML files

find /var -type f -size +100M -exec ls -lh {} \; → show large files with details

find /etc -name "*nginx*" 2>/dev/null → search nginx-related configs (ignore errors)

find /etc -name "*.conf" -mtime -1 → find recently modified config files

⚡ Whether it’s debugging logs, finding disk hogs, or auditing configs – find is a must-know tool for DevOps & Linux admins.
